{'text': 'Reducing the likelihood that a wildfire burning in adjoining vegetation will ignite homes or other structures isone ofthe more effective avenues to reducing losses. Individual homeowners can take many actions, but others require concerted effort atthe community level tobe effective. Similarly, community efforts without commensurate attention by local home and business owners are unlikely to succeed. Therefore, actions by property owners to reduce the ignitability of homes and other structures are prudent wherever structures are near flammable vegetation. Data onthe incidence of buildings involved in outdoor fires suggest that essentially all communities would benefit from more attention by property owners. Beyond that first step, there are areas of higher risk where additional emphasis on home or community efforts might be placed. 7 Stein, S.M., J. Menakis, M.A. Carr, S.J. Comas, S.I. Stewart, H. Cleveland, L. Bramwell, andV.C. Radeloff. 2013. Wildfire, wildlands, and people: understanding and preparing for wildfire inthe wildland-urban interface—a Forests onthe Edge report. Gen. Tech. Rep. RMRS-GTR-299. Fort Collins, CO. U.S. Department of Agriculture, Forest Service, Rocky Mountain Research Station. 36p.This home was lost because ithad not proactively reduced risks posed by wildfire through homeowner action and hazardous fuels reduction before the Wallow Fire in Arizona, in 2011. Photo credit: Kari Greer. This home was saved because itwas located ina thinned area which provided safety to firefighters who burned the ground fuels around itas the Wallow Fire approached. Photo credit: Kari Greer. Recent patterns of structures lost or buildings involved in incidents help identify areas of possible prioritization.', 'source': 'The National Strategy.pdf', 'topic': 'National cohesive wildland fire management strategy for the United States', 'region': 'United States', 'source_name': 'The National Strategy: The Final Phase in the Development of the National Cohesive Wildland Fire Management Strategy', 'language': 'English', 'summary': "This report, 'The National Strategy', is the culmination of a three-phased, intergovernmental planning and analysis process for a national cohesive wildland fire management strategy in the United States, as mandated by the Federal Land Assistance, Management, and Enhancement (FLAME) Act of 2009 [146-148]. It outlines three primary goals: restore and maintain landscapes, create fire-adapted communities, and improve wildfire response [147]. The strategy addresses national challenges, categorized into vegetation and fuels, homes/communities and other values at risk, human-caused ignitions, and effective and efficient wildfire response [149-152]. It includes regional characterizations for the Northeast, Southeast, and West, detailing diverse ecosystems, land ownership patterns, fire season characteristics, and factors influencing wildfire threats [153-155]. The document emphasizes the importance of collaboration among federal, state, local, and tribal governments and non-governmental partners [146, 156, 157], and how data analysis and spatially explicit priorities can inform efforts such as grant funding for wildfire risk mitigation [158]."}

